<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Operation Sheepskin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Operation Sheepskin was the British landing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anguilla">Anguilla</a> on 19 March 1969 to end the island's self-declared break from the associated state of Saint Kitts-Nevis-Anguilla. Some 300 paratroopers and a contingent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Metropolitan Police officers arrived unopposed, and the operation was widely ridiculed in the press. Anguilla then came under direct British administration and was formally separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts-and-nevis">Saint Kitts and Nevis</a> in 1980.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 27 February 1967 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> made Saint Christopher-Nevis-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anguilla">Anguilla</a> an associated state with its own constitution and wide self-government. Anguilla, poor and arid, lacked electricity, clean water and adequate roads, and its people felt that most aid went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> and resented the Chief Minister, Robert Llewellyn Bradshaw, who dismissed their concerns. After riots and clashes, the Kittitian police were forcibly evicted on 30 May 1967, remembered as Anguilla Day. An 18-man raid on Saint Kitts on 9 June failed, and on 11 July a referendum backed secession by 1,813 votes to five.</p><p>A conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a> later in July, led by Lord Shepherd, kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anguilla">Anguilla</a> tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> and was rejected on the island under the more militant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-webster">Ronald Webster</a>. From December 1967 the British civil servant Tony Lee administered the island under an interim agreement, which came to be seen as a delay before reintegration. On 6 February 1969 a referendum voted by 1,739 to 4 to cut all ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and form a republic. On 11 March the junior minister William Whitlock arrived with proposals; his curt manner offended the islanders, and he and his party were ejected at gunpoint.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 19 March 1969 about 300 soldiers of the 2nd Battalion, Parachute Regiment, with 9 Independent Parachute Squadron RE and 22 officers of the London Metropolitan Police, came ashore by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a> and landing craft from two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> frigates. Not a shot was fired. The troops were met by foreign journalists and islanders, some of them indignant, and found none of the intimidation, mafia presence or expected firearms that Whitlock had reported; most guns had been hidden. The soldiers ran a hearts and minds campaign, and after six weeks the first paratroopers flew home, leaving B Company until 14 September 1969.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The operation drew ridicule at home and abroad, and the world press dubbed it the Bay of Piglets. The public relations embarrassment contributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-wilson">Harold Wilson</a>'s defeat at the 1970 general election. Civil strife was limited after the landing, and the islanders came to accept the political situation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anguilla">Anguilla</a> formally seceded from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts-and-nevis">Saint Kitts and Nevis</a> in 1980, Saint Kitts and Nevis became independent in 1983, and Anguilla remained a British territory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Operation Sheepskin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 19 March 1969 British paratroopers and London police landed unopposed in <b>Operation Sheepskin</b>, an episode widely mocked in the press.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-anguilla">Republic of Anguilla</a></li></ul></section>
